/-
# Maximal periodic subgroups of integral linear groups (Kourovka Notebook Problem 1.73)

Kourovka Notebook, Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.73 (V. P. Platonov): are there only
finitely many conjugacy classes of maximal periodic subgroups in a finitely generated linear group
over the integers?
-/

module

public import Batteries.Util.ProofWanted
public import Mathlib.LinearAlgebra.Matrix.GeneralLinearGroup.Defs

@[expose] public section

namespace MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MaximalPeriodicSubgroupsIntegralLinearKourovka1Problem73Wanted

/-!
Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov (eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka
Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49 (2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.73 (V. P.
Platonov). UnsolvedMath record UM-00700064 (`KOU-1.73`), dataset ulamai/UnsolvedMath v1.8.0.

A finitely generated linear group over the integers is a finitely generated subgroup `G` of some
`GL_n(ℤ)`. A periodic subgroup has all elements of finite order; maximal periodic subgroups of `G`
and their conjugacy are taken inside `G`.
-/

/-- The subgroup `H` is periodic: every element has finite order. -/
def IsPeriodic {G : Type} [Group G] (H : Subgroup G) : Prop :=
  ∀ x ∈ H, IsOfFinOrder x

/-- `H` is a maximal periodic subgroup: periodic and contained in no strictly larger periodic
subgroup. -/
def IsMaximalPeriodic {G : Type} [Group G] (H : Subgroup G) : Prop :=
  IsPeriodic H ∧ ∀ K : Subgroup G, IsPeriodic K → H ≤ K → K = H

/-- Kourovka 1.73 (V. P. Platonov): in every finitely generated subgroup `G` of some `GL_n(ℤ)`, the
maximal periodic subgroups fall into finitely many conjugacy classes: there is a finite set `F` of
subgroups such that every maximal periodic subgroup is `g K g⁻¹` for some `K ∈ F` and `g ∈ G`. -/
def finitelyManyMaximalPeriodicClasses : Prop :=
  ∀ (n : ℕ) (G : Subgroup (GL (Fin n) ℤ)), G.FG →
    ∃ F : Set (Subgroup G), F.Finite ∧ ∀ H : Subgroup G, IsMaximalPeriodic H →
      ∃ K ∈ F, ∃ g : G, ∀ x : G, x ∈ H ↔ g⁻¹ * x * g ∈ K

/--
Resolved false: the Kourovka Archive answers 1.73 "Not always" with Merzlyakov's counterexample: `H
= F(a, b) ⋊ ⟨φ⟩`, `φ : a ↦ a⁻¹, b ↦ b`, is linear over the integers, and the subgroup `G ≤ H × H'`
generated by `a`, `φ`, `a'φ'`, `bb'` contains the maximal periodic subgroups `T_n = ⟨c₀, c_n'⟩`,
`c_n = φ b⁻ⁿ a bⁿ`, which are pairwise non-conjugate in `G`. Source: E. I. Khukhro and V. D. Mazurov
(eds.), Unsolved Problems in Group Theory: The Kourovka Notebook, 21st issue, arXiv:1401.0300v49
(2026), Archive of Solved Problems, Problem 1.73 (V. P. Platonov); resolution: Yu. I. Merzlyakov
(1973), as recorded in the Kourovka Archive, Problem 1.73, Archive answer: Not always; G ≤ H × H'
generated by a, φ, a'φ', bb', with H the extension of F(a, b) by φ : a ↦ a⁻¹, b ↦ b, contains
pairwise non-conjugate maximal periodic subgroups T_n = ⟨c₀, c_n'⟩,
https://arxiv.org/abs/1401.0300v49.
-/
public theorem_wanted finitelyManyMaximalPeriodicClasses_refuted :
    ¬ finitelyManyMaximalPeriodicClasses

end MathlibExt.GroupTheory.MaximalPeriodicSubgroupsIntegralLinearKourovka1Problem73Wanted
